# Servers

- `internal/servers.Service` sits between the handlers and the store/docker client. Handlers stay thin: decode, call the service, map errors with `serversError` (`FieldError` → `validation_failed`, sentinels → `not_found`/`name_taken`/`port_in_use`/`conflict`, `ErrRunning` → `conflict`, `ErrAdminUnavailable` and `docker.ErrUnavailable` → 503).
- A server runs a build (`store.Server.BuildID`): create/patch accept only `success` builds with an image (`resolveBuild`, no Docker call); `start` re-reads the build and verifies its `image_id` with `docker.BuildImage(buildRef(b))` (managed + instance + exactly the build's labels, like `BuiltImage` at the end of the build) before `docker run`. Never accept an image reference from a request again. `project_id`, `image`, `commit`, `engine_version` in views come from the build (`buildCache`); the legacy `servers.project_id` column is unused.
- A server's container is `store.Server.ContainerID`, recorded by `start` right after `docker run` together with the admin host port and the sealed admin token (`SetServerContainer`), cleared with them by `removeRecorded`; every docker call uses `ref(sv)`. Containers with our labels that are not recorded are ignored and logged once (`logForeign`). No recorded id means `state: "missing"` even when Docker is down.
- The engine deploy contract is `engineEnv` (`engineenv.go`, variable names from golem-engine `config_env.go`/`host/env.go`): passed as `RunSpec.DaemonEnv`, overriding operator env; the public and fallback URLs use the build's transport paths (`transportPaths`: recorded by the build, engine defaults for older rows). `configHash` covers the build, run settings, env ciphertext hash, `engineEnv` without the token (game host, allowed origins, certificate mode), the certificate mount and groups; bump `envContractVersion` when the variables change meaning. `runtimeParams` (game host via `GameHost`: setting, else `Config.DefaultGameHost`; certificate mode/dir/groups from `Config.Certs`) is read once per request.
- Env is a JSON object sealed with `secrets.ServerEnvPurpose(id)`, the admin token with `secrets.ServerAdminTokenPurpose(id)` (fresh per container, `newAdminToken`); only `env_keys` leave the service. Never log env values or the token. Container output shown to users goes through `sanitizer` (both `/logs` and job logs; job logs add `JobLogPrefix`), and the failed-start excerpt is `redact`ed first (operator values and the token). Tests plant a marker value and grep responses, job details, job logs, daemon logs, docker argv and CLI env (`assertNoMarker`, `assertNoAdminToken`); keep that coverage for new code paths.
- Starts are health-gated (`waitReady`): poll the container and `gameadmin.Client.Ready` every `ReadyPoll` until ready, exit or `StartTimeout`; failures stop the container (kept, no restart policy) and return user-safe `jobs.Fail` messages; on ready, `/info`'s fingerprint goes to `store.SetBuildFingerprint` and `ApplyRestartPolicy` runs. `Runtime` (GET `/servers/{id}/runtime`) re-reads the admin port from inspect (Docker may reassign it) and maps admin failures to `ErrAdminUnavailable`.
- `internal/gameadmin` is the only code that talks to a game: 127.0.0.1 only, fixed dialer, no proxy, no redirects, no compression, `RequestTimeout`, `MaxResponseBytes`, every value through `Clean`/`Fingerprint`/`Transport`/clamps. `gameadmintest` is the fake endpoint (hostile modes) and `BuildFakeGameImage` (the static `fakegame` program in a FROM scratch image) for Docker integration tests.
- Ports come from the configured range; auto-assignment retries on UNIQUE conflicts; `portAllowed` (range, unsafe list, `PanelPort`) is re-checked when a start job runs. A port change while the container runs is `ErrRunning`. Allowed origins are validated by `ValidateOrigin` (normalised, at most `MaxAllowedOrigins`).
- `Update` writes only build/port/origins/env via `store.UpdateServer` (env compare-and-swapped, retried on `store.ErrStale`); `desired_state` is written only by `SetServerDesiredState` in jobs, the container columns only by jobs.
- Start/stop/restart/delete are job bodies (`Start`, `Stop`, `Restart`, `Delete`) run via `startServerJob` (which passes the job id with `WithJobID`) with subject `server`, so one job per server runs at a time; Docker failures go through `failDocker` (fixed message per class, raw error to the daemon log with `job_id`). DELETE waits for its `server.delete` job.
- Reads take runtime state from one `ManagedContainers` call per list. A failed runtime query goes through `dockerFailed`: Docker errors are logged and reported as `unknown`, but a query cancelled by its caller logs at debug and ends the request as cancelled.
- Export/import (format 2) reference builds as `{project, commit}`: import resolves `store.LatestSuccessfulBuild` per entry and needs no Docker. The import route has `maxBody: servers.MaxImportBytes` (keep `MaxImportServers` × worst-case escaped env and origins below it; `TestD14_ImportRoundTripAtMaximum`). Images: `in_use` when a successful build records the image (`store.BuildsByImage`) or any container uses it (`docker.ImagesInUse`), the builds rule's single image rule; such images cannot be deleted through `/images` (delete the build).
- `Reconcile` (called by the daemon at startup) starts servers with desired state `running` whose recorded container is not running and only logs unrecorded containers.
- Static uploads: per-account slots (`static.BeginUpload`), `Stage` bounded by `static.Budget()`, one `Commit` per request; quota/no-space errors map to `507 insufficient_storage`; deleting a file a project uses as a static add-on is `409` (builds rule).
- Verify with `go test ./internal/servers/... ./internal/gameadmin/... ./internal/httpapi/... ./internal/daemon/...` (findings regressions are `TestD*`); `GOLEM_DEPLOY_DOCKER_TESTS=1` adds `TestIntegrationServersAPI` (needs the go toolchain to compile the fake game).
